<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reinhard Heydrich</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1904">1904</a>–<a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Reinhard Heydrich was a senior SS officer, head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reich-security-main-office">Reich Security Main Office</a> and one of the main architects of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>, who chaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wannsee-conference">Wannsee Conference</a> in January 1942. Appointed deputy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reich-protector">Reich Protector</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> in 1941, he ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> by terror. Czechoslovak parachutists mortally wounded him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in May 1942, and the Nazis destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lidice">Lidice</a> in reprisal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reinhard-heydrich.jpg" alt="Reinhard Heydrich" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uncredited photographer. US National Archives and Records Administration (NARA): Unrestricted access and use. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reinhard_Heydrich_1940_Portrait_(3x4_cropped)_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Reinhard Tristan Eugen Heydrich was born on 7 March 1904 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halle-saale">Halle an der Saale</a>. His father, Richard Bruno Heydrich, was a composer and opera singer who founded the Halle Conservatory of Music, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> and Teaching, and his mother taught piano there. Heydrich was trained in music from an early age and kept up the violin into adulthood. In 1922 he joined the German Navy as a cadet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a> and became a first lieutenant in July 1928. His discharge from the navy took legal effect on 30 May 1931. He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> that year, and the SS on 14 July 1931.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1931 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-himmler">Heinrich Himmler</a> hired him to set up an intelligence service for the SS, and in mid-1932 Heydrich became chief of the Sicherheitsdienst (SD). On 22 April 1934 Himmler named him head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a>. With Himmler, Göring and others he drew up the lists of those to be killed in the purge of the SA on 30 June 1934, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/night-of-the-long-knives">Night of the Long Knives</a>. In 1938 he coordinated the pogrom of Kristallnacht on 9–10 November by telegram to SD and Gestapo offices, authorising arson and the arrest of as many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> as the detention facilities could hold. From September 1939 he led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reich-security-main-office">Reich Security Main Office</a>.</p><p>On 27 September 1941 he was appointed Deputy Reich Protector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, replacing the policies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstantin-von-neurath">Konstantin von Neurath</a>. Heydrich proclaimed martial law, and 142 people were executed within five days of his arrival in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>. In January 1942 he chaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wannsee-conference">Wannsee Conference</a>, which formalised plans for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> and murder of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in German-occupied Europe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Czechoslovak government-in-exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> resolved to kill him, and Jan Kubiš and Jozef Gabčík, trained by the British Special Operations Executive, parachuted into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> on 28 December 1941. Heydrich was ambushed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> and mortally wounded on 27 May 1942 in Operation Anthropoid, and died of sepsis on 4 June. German intelligence falsely linked the assassins to the villages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lidice">Lidice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezaky">Ležáky</a>. Both villages were razed, the men and boys aged 14 and over were shot, and most of the women and children were deported and murdered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a>. Hitler called Heydrich the man with the iron heart, and many historians regard him as one of the most sinister figures of the Nazi regime.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Reinhard Heydrich” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The occupation brought the persecution and murder of most of the country's Jews, while the assassination of <b>Reinhard Heydrich</b> in 1942 and the destruction of Lidice in reprisal became symbols of resistance and terror.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Terror was organised by the SS under Heinrich Himmler, which absorbed the police, ran the Gestapo and the concentration camps from Dachau, opened in March 1933, onwards, and in 1939 combined its security services in the Reich Security Main Office under <b>Reinhard Heydrich</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Chaired by <b>Reinhard Heydrich</b>, senior officials met at a villa on the Wannsee in Berlin to organise the 'Final Solution', the deportation and murder of Europe's Jews.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-20">20 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A Czech government under President Emil Hácha remained in place, but real power lay with the Reich Protector, first Konstantin von Neurath and then <b>Reinhard Heydrich</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/protectorate-of-bohemia-and-moravia">Protectorate of Bohemia and Moravia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halle-saale">Halle (Saale)</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.48,11.97&amp;year=1928" aria-label="Halle (Saale) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Reinhard Heydrich"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5633 5668h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5633 5668h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.48,11.97&amp;year=1928">What was here in 1928? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
